<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Describe first and later glucose readings, distinguish change from slope, handle
unobserved threshold attainment, and interpret a model with only ten people.
</div>

**You need:** the Opal login from [Getting started](book-00-start.html) and
[the lab lesson](book-14-labs.html). This chapter uses `omop.synthea`.

## Research question

Among people with blood-glucose measurements, how do values change over recorded
follow-up? This is a lab-defined population, not a diagnosis-confirmed diabetes
cohort. The Synthea data are synthetic; the cutoffs below are teaching rules.

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.synthea", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("numeric")$conns
```

## Choose a populated measurement

```{r glucose-discovery}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Glucose", domain = "Measurement", vocabulary = "LOINC", datasources = conns)$pooled
hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
glucose_id <- hits$concept_id[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "Glucose [Mass/volume] in Blood"]
ds.omop.concept.locate(glucose_id, type = "both", datasources = conns)$pooled
ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id", concept_id = glucose_id,
  type = "both", datasources = conns)$pooled[c("value", "concept_name", "n")]
glucose_set <- omop_concept_set(glucose_id)
saveRDS(glucose_set, "glucose-concept.rds")
```

Blood glucose is **3000483**, recorded in **8840**, milligram per deciliter
(mg/dL). The locator shows a band of 225–229 rows and 10–14 people. The separate
Serum or Plasma concept has records from only one person in the public synthetic
source; its occurrence cannot support a released analysis. Do not combine
specimens merely to increase the sample.

## Select a baseline and a genuine later value

Index on first glucose. The last-value window starts the following day, so a
person with only a baseline reading cannot supply a follow-up outcome.
A slope uses all readings and their actual spacing; last minus first ignores
how long the change took.

```{r glucose-recipe, table.digits = 3}
glucose <- omop_event("measurement", glucose_set, unit = omop_unit(8840L))
first <- omop_variable(event = glucose, reducer = omop_reduce("first_value", window = omop_window(0, 0)))
last <- omop_variable(event = glucose, reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", window = omop_window(1, NULL)))
cohort <- omop_population("measured", index = omop_index(glucose))
trajectory <- omop_recipe(populations = list(cohort), variables = list(
  first = first, last = last,
  control = omop_number_bins(last, breaks = c(-Inf, 90, Inf), labels = c("below 90", "90 or above")),
  change = omop_number_change(glucose),
  per_year = omop_number(omop_number_change(glucose, "slope", time_unit = "days"),
    omop_number_spec(transform = omop_number_transform("affine", factor = 365.25))),
  followup_days = omop_variable(event = glucose, reducer = omop_reduce("last_date", window = omop_window(1, NULL))),
  target_days = omop_number_change(glucose, "time_to_threshold", threshold = 95, op = "ge")),
  outputs = omop_output("people", rows = "measured", format = "wide"))
recipe_execute(trajectory, out = c(people = "D_glucose"), datasources = conns)
sapply(c("first", "last", "change", "per_year", "followup_days"), function(column)
  dsBaseClient::ds.quantileMean(paste0("D_glucose$", column), datasources = conns))
```

All ten people have later values, with median follow-up about 18 years.
First and last means are 83.40 and 85.98 mg/dL; mean change is 2.58 mg/dL.
Mean slope is 0.397 mg/dL per year (median 0.221). A change over many years is not a short-term treatment response, and a
straight-line slope can miss fluctuations between readings.

## Classify values and distinguish unobserved attainment

```{r glucose-control}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_glucose$control",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Four people are below 90 and six are at least 90 mg/dL.
It is a presentation rule, not a diagnosis or treatment goal. More detailed
categories may be withheld in a population this small.

For a separate timing question, use the first **observed** glucose ≥95 mg/dL.
In this tiny synthetic dataset, a 100 mg/dL rule would leave too few people for a
released summary, so the lesson uses 95 mg/dL as a teaching threshold. In a real
study the threshold comes from the protocol, and a withheld result is reported
as withheld, not avoided by changing the cutoff.

```{r glucose-target, table.digits = 3}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_glucose$target_days", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.numNA("D_glucose$target_days", datasources = conns)
```

Eight people have an observed attainment and two are missing. The median among
attainments is 1,169 days; the mean is 2,101.5 days. One of the eight attains at
day zero, which is not shown directly by these interpolated quantiles.
A zero means the threshold was met at baseline; a positive time means a later
recorded crossing. Missing means no crossing was observed during available
measurements. The missing people must remain under observation until censoring
in a time-to-event analysis; a mean among observed crossings alone is conditional
on crossing and does not estimate everyone's time to threshold.

## Model last against first with appropriate uncertainty

A Gaussian regression describes the association between first and last readings.
With ten complete people and two coefficients, use a t interval with eight
residual degrees of freedom rather than the large-sample z interval.

```{r glucose-model, warning=FALSE, table.digits = 3}
fit <- dsBaseClient::ds.glm("last ~ first", data = "D_glucose", family = "gaussian", datasources = conns)
b <- fit$coefficients["first", "Estimate"]
se <- fit$coefficients["first", "Std. Error"]
data.frame(N = fit$Nvalid, df = fit$df, Estimate = b, Lower_95 = b - qt(.975, fit$df) * se,
  Upper_95 = b + qt(.975, fit$df) * se, P_t = 2 * pt(-abs(b / se), df = fit$df))
```

The coefficient is about −0.75: a 1 mg/dL higher first value is associated with
about 0.75 mg/dL lower last value. The t-based interval includes zero: the
95% interval is −1.616 to 0.119 and the t-based p-value is 0.082. Regression to the mean, unequal follow-up and the sample
of only ten people make this association fragile; it is not evidence that high
baseline glucose causes improvement.

## Exercises

**1. Change the time unit.** Express the observed threshold times in years and
retain the number with missing attainment.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r glucose-years, table.digits = 3}
ds.omop.mutate("D_glucose", target_years = target_days / 365.25,
  newobj = "D_glucose_years", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_glucose_years$target_years", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.numNA("D_glucose_years$target_years", datasources = conns)
```

The median becomes 3.20 years and the mean 5.75 years. Divide the day summary
by 365.25 to check the values. The valid and missing
counts do not change: rescaling cannot supply unobserved crossings.

</details>

**2. Separate endpoints from a trajectory.** Calculate last minus first on the
server and compare with the native change summary. Does agreement prove a steady increase?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r glucose-difference, table.digits = 3}
ds.omop.mutate("D_glucose", difference = last - first,
  newobj = "D_difference", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_difference$difference", datasources = conns)
```

The difference agrees with native change because both calculate last minus
first. All ten people have later readings, so the complete-case populations also
coincide. It describes only two endpoints; intermediate values can rise and fall.
The slope uses those intermediate observations but still assumes a straight line.

</details>

## Recap

Retain real follow-up, state the time scale, and distinguish observed crossings
from censoring. Small datasets can support a useful descriptive analysis without
supporting a precise or clinically predictive model.

**Next:** [22. Case study: drug utilisation](book-22-case-drug.html).
